*Cosa Le Cun was born in the dead chill of the Lord Reverend’s Winter, when the bodies of the dead still clogged St. Farro’s Street and the red stain on the snow in Grace Square had not yet been buried beneath fresh snow. He was born sickly and cold, and his mother did not think he would survive to spring.*  

*On the day the Lord Reverend fell from Le Coron, the young abbess of the Kind Sisters of Le Vel opened her doors to discover a baby, wrapped in cheap but clean linens. While the rest of the castoffs and crooks of Le Vel scavenged the venerable Lord Revenerd’s corpse, the Kind Sisters took instead to the care of the poor boy. They fed him goat’s milk, and later mashed potatoes and liver, and he grew strong. He lived to see the spring.*  

*The young abbess named him Cosaro, after the wisest and most blessed of the old Emperors, but all the sisters called him little Cosa. They hoped that one day, he might live up to his namesake.*  

*At first, he slept in the kitchens with the other orphans. The Lord Reverend had made many orphans during his brief but terrible rule over the city, and the Kind Sisters had taken responsibility for them. But as Cosa grew older, he took to sleeping in the kennels with the guard hounds, where it was quiet. Though Cosa would one day claim otherwise, the epithet ‘Le Cun’ was bestowed upon him for this reason, and no other. The other orphans in the abbey, and a few of the least kind Kind Sisters, said he smelled like a dog, and thus he must be one. He fought it, at first, but the resistance proved impossible, so instead he made it a point of pride.* 

*For a time, he was a happy little boy. The sisters gave the orphans chores and taught them letters and numbers as well as many other practical matters. But as he grew older, little Cosa grew restless. The abbess, not old but not so young anymore, often said he had a little fire in his soul that, if he sat still too long, would burn him up. Cosa felt no fire, but he knew that the streets sang to him at night, beckoning him out from the warmth and safety of the abbey and into the wild beauty of Le Vel.* 

*In the spring of four hundredth and forty third year since the Grace, Cosa Le Cun ran away from the only home he had ever known. The very next day, he met Sabon.*